<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/human-body-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The human body is <strong>eleven interlocking systems built from ~37 trillion
    cells</strong>, each system a specialist service for the whole. Most of those cells sit
    far from any source of food or oxygen — a cell in your calf is a metre from the air you
    breathe — so something must deliver to them constantly, or they die within minutes. That
    something is the circulatory system, and it is this lesson's spine: the body's
    transport network, from the pump (heart) to the pipes (vessels) to the cargo (blood).
    The unifying theme across all eleven systems is <strong>homeostasis</strong>: keeping
    temperature, pH, sugar and salt within narrow ranges despite a chaotic world. Health is
    homeostasis holding; disease is it slipping; medicine is mostly helping it back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Circulation is the clearest place to see homeostasis working, because the blood is the
    medium every other system's output travels in — hormones from the endocrine system,
    heat from muscles, glucose from digestion, antibodies from the immune system. Read the
    transport system well and you have read the body's operating manual.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the systems, grouped by what they do for the whole" widget="equationStep" config={bodyCfg} />

  <h2>Circulatory systems: the design space</h2>
  <p>
    Simple animals skip the problem: a flatworm is thin enough that diffusion alone feeds
    every cell. Anything thicker needs a dedicated fluid, driven in bulk — a
    <strong>circulatory system</strong>. Two design decisions define all of them. The first
    is <strong>open versus closed</strong>. In an open system (insects, most molluscs) the
    blood — called haemolymph — spills from vessels into a body cavity and sloshes around
    the organs before being drawn back; it is cheap and fine for low demands. In a closed
    system (vertebrates, earthworms, squid) the blood stays inside vessels throughout,
    held at high pressure and routed precisely — the design that supports big, active
    bodies. The second is <strong>single versus double circulation</strong>. Fish have a
    single loop: heart → gills → body → heart, one pump, and blood loses pressure picking
    up oxygen, which is part of why fish are limited to water. Birds and mammals run a
    <strong>double circulation</strong>: the right heart drives a low-pressure loop to the
    lungs, the left heart a high-pressure loop to the body — two pumps in series, so the
    body loop never suffers the pressure drop of the lung stopover. That split is what
    makes mammalian-sized, warm-blooded, high-metabolism life possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the heart pumps blood around and that's one loop."
    Trace a red cell: right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left
    ventricle → body → back to the right atrium. It passes through the heart <em>twice</em>
    per lap, and the two halves are pumping into separate circuits — which is why the
    left ventricle's wall is ~3× thicker than the right's.
  </p>

  <h2>The heart: a self-wiring double pump</h2>
  <p>
    The heart is a fist of cardiac muscle with four chambers and four one-way valves.
    <strong>Atria</strong> receive; <strong>ventricles</strong> eject; the tricuspid and
    mitral valves guard the atrium–ventricle doors, the pulmonic and aortic valves the
    exits. The beat runs on its own built-in electrical system: the
    <strong>sinoatrial node</strong> — a patch of self-firing cells in the right atrium —
    sparks every ~0.8 s at rest, the wave sweeps the atria, and the
    <strong>atrioventricular node</strong> relays it to the ventricles after a built-in
    delay so the upper chambers finish emptying first. "Lub-dub" is the sound of valves
    slamming: first the atrioventricular pair as the ventricles contract
    (<strong>systole</strong>), then the outlet valves as they refill
    (<strong>diastole</strong>). Output is the product you will meet again in the medicine
    lesson: <strong>cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</strong> — 72 beats/min ×
    70 mL ≈ 5 L/min at rest, and up to 20–30 L/min in trained athletes, most of the extra
    coming from a bigger stroke volume: exercise empties the ventricles harder and fills
    them deeper.
  </p>
  <p>
    The heart feeds itself first: <strong>coronary arteries</strong> branch off the aorta's
    very first centimetre, because the muscle cannot borrow from the blood it pumps through
    its own interior chambers. Block them — atherosclerosis, the gradual furring of arteries
    with fatty plaque — and the muscle downstream starves: angina when partial, a heart
    attack (myocardial infarction) when complete. It remains the world's leading cause of
    death, and its big four risk factors — smoking, high blood pressure, bad lipids,
    inactivity — are the modifiable ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood vessels: three pipes, three jobs</h2>
  <p>
    Vessels are a study in structure matching function. <strong>Arteries</strong> carry
    blood away from the heart at high pressure: thick, elastic walls stretch with each
    pulse and recoil between beats, smoothing intermittent ejection into continuous flow
    (feel your pulse — that is the artery wall recoiling). <strong>Arterioles</strong> are
    the adjustable taps whose smooth-muscle rings route blood — to skin for cooling, gut
    after lunch, muscles in a sprint. <strong>Capillaries</strong> are the destination:
    one endothelial cell thick, lumen barely wider than a red cell, so flow slows to
    single-file and exchange has time to happen — every other vessel exists to feed these.
    <strong>Veins</strong> return at low pressure: thin walls, large capacity, and a
    problem — how does blood get uphill from your feet? Valves prevent backflow while your
    skeletal muscles squeeze the veins (the "muscle pump"; this is why standing still for
    hours pools blood in your legs and why long flights raise clot risk). Varicose veins
    are failed valves; deep-vein thrombosis is a clot where you can least afford one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Blood pressure</strong> reads as systole-over-diastole, 120/80 mmHg the classic
    resting reference. Chronic hypertension is the "silent" risk factor because the
    suffering is deferred — the heart thickens, arteries stiffen, small vessels in kidneys,
    retina and brain give out first.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood: the cargo and the crew</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is a tissue: ~55% <strong>plasma</strong> (water, proteins, glucose, hormones,
    antibodies, wastes) and ~45% cells. <strong>Red cells</strong> (erythrocytes) are
    concave discs, ejected from marrow without a nucleus, each packing ~270 million
    haemoglobin molecules — oxygen taxis binding O₂ in the lungs and releasing it where
    levels drop. Each lives ~120 days; the marrow replaces ~2 million per second.
    <strong>White cells</strong> (leucocytes) are the immune crew — neutrophils as
    fast-response bacteria hunters, lymphocytes as the targeted intelligence service behind
    antibodies and vaccination. <strong>Platelets</strong> are cell fragments that trigger
    clotting: a cascade that turns exposed vessel wall into a fibrin plug in minutes —
    life-saving at a cut, lethal inside a coronary artery. <em>Blood groups</em> (the ABO
    and rhesus systems) are inherited surface-sugar variants; transplant the wrong one and
    the recipient's antibodies destroy the gift — which is why a donated unit is typed,
    cross-matched and traced from vein to vein.
  </p>

  <h2>The other systems, and homeostasis holding</h2>
  <p>
    The widget groups the remaining systems; two homeostasis loops show how they talk.
    <strong>Thermoregulation:</strong> the hypothalamus is the thermostat — sweat when hot
    (evaporative cooling), shiver when cold (muscle-generated heat), skin vessels flush or
    clamp — holding core temperature within about 1 °C of 37 °C. <strong>Blood sugar:</strong>
    after a meal the pancreas releases <strong>insulin</strong>, which tells cells to take
    up glucose and the liver to store it; between meals <strong>glucagon</strong> tells the
    liver to release it. Two hormones in a push–pull loop keep blood glucose near 5 mmol/L;
    <strong>diabetes</strong> is this loop failing (type 1: the insulin-producing cells are
    destroyed; type 2: the targets stop answering), and injected insulin — isolated in 1921
    — remains one of medicine's great saves. Note the pattern in both loops:
    <strong>negative feedback</strong> (output cancels the deviation) stabilises, which is
    most of physiology; <strong>positive feedback</strong> drives rare events to completion
    — childbirth amplifies its own contractions, clotting amplifies itself — because an
    event, not a level, is wanted.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the body's residents outnumber its cells: ~38 trillion bacteria live on and in you
    (the microbiome), aiding digestion, synthesising vitamins, training the immune system —
    the "you" being kept alive is an ecosystem.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the transport system is built with headroom. Blood volume is ~5 L and a
    full circuit takes about a minute at rest (the lung loop ~10 seconds), so the same litre
    of blood makes ~1,400 circuits a day — about 7,300 L moved, pushed by a pump that
    consumes ~5% of the body's energy budget for a lifetime of ~2.5–3 billion beats. Oxygen
    delivery is cardiac output × haemoglobin content × saturation; at rest it roughly
    quadruples actual demand, and the reserve is what exercise draws on — VO₂max, the
    ceiling, is the strongest single predictor of all-cause mortality in cohort studies.
    The adaptations run both ways: endurance training grows stroke volume (and thickens the
    left ventricle's <em>cavity</em>, unlike the wall-thickening of hypertension), and
    detraining reverses it within weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparative physiology sharpens the design argument. A giraffe stands ~5 m tall, so its
    brain sits ~2 m above its heart, demanding a blood pressure near 280/180 mmHg at the
    pump — held in check by tight leg-fascia and one-way valves against oedema, a natural
    experiment in hypertension management. Diving mammals (seals, whales) run reflex
    <em>diving bradycardia</em> — heart rates crashing from ~100 to under 10 beats/min,
    blood shunted to brain and heart alone — the same selective-routing machinery your
    arterioles use, taken to its limit. And the transplant frontier shows what the
    remaining margin is: hearts can be cooled and stopped for a few hours (organ
    preservation), but a fully artificial replacement that beats 3 billion times without
    clotting or wearing remains unsolved engineering — the biological pump still sets the
    standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    A third loop deserves a place because it couples the transport system to the
    excretory one: <strong>osmoregulation</strong>. The blood arriving at the kidneys is
    monitored for water and salt content; too concentrated, and the pituitary releases
    <strong>ADH</strong>, which makes the kidney tubules reabsorb more water — dark,
    scanty urine on a dehydrated day; too dilute, and ADH falls — pale, copious urine
    after a large drink. The loop is negative feedback like blood sugar's, but note its
    speed and its seat: the correction runs through the same bloodstream the transport
    system moves, and the whole fluid volume of the body is retuned within hours. The
    kidney filters ~180 L of blood daily to reclaim ~178.5 L of it — transport at its
    most profligate and precise, and the reason the "waste disposal" system is really a
    homeostasis organ that happens to produce urine.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Anatomy and Physiology 2e; Cambridge IGCSE Biology syllabus (section 9); Guyton &amp; Hall (clinical reference); Sender, Fuchs &amp; Milo, PNAS (2016) on cell counts.</p>
